---
title: Export Statistics
sidebar:
  order: 13
---

Use the statistics export when you need a summary table rather than the full
records. The tables are counts and percentages by category, which is what most
grant reports, permit reports, and progress updates ask for. Every table shown
on the statistics screen can be written to CSV, TSV, Excel, or JSON.

Statistics are calculated for the active project only.

## Opening the statistics screen

Open the record statistics panel on the project dashboard and select
`Explore more stats`. The screen has three parts:

- **Summary** shows the project totals, split into a `Record summary` group
  and a `Taxonomic breakdown` group that counts distinct classes, orders,
  families, genera, and species, followed by the top five counts by category.
- **Detailed statistics** builds one table or chart from the measure, grouping,
  breakdown, and filter you choose.
- **Spatial statistics** counts records by site coordinate, shown on a map or
  as a table.

Exports are available from the table view. Switch a panel from `Chart` or `Map`
to `Table`, then use the `Export table` button above the table.

## Detailed statistics

Choose a `Measure` first, because it decides which groupings are available.

| Measure | Group by |
| --- | --- |
| Specimens | Species, Taxon rank, Site, Date, Method, Sex, Life stage |
| Species | Taxon rank, Site, Date, Method, Sex, Life stage |
| Part quantity | Part type, Treatment |

Life stage only appears when the project records it. Three further controls
appear when they apply:

- **Taxon rank** picks the rank to count by: `Class`, `Order`, `Family`, or
  `Genus`. Species has its own grouping, because it is the only one that
  offers the breakdown and the site filter below. The chart title, the table
  header, and the file name all name the rank you picked rather than the
  grouping, so counting by family reads `Specimens by family`.
- **Breakdown** splits specimen counts by species into `Sex` or `Life stage`.
  The breakdown becomes an extra column in the exported table.
- **Filters** narrow the table to one site when counting specimens by species,
  or to one species when counting part quantity. The selected filter is
  included in the default file name.

The exported table repeats what the on-screen table shows, one row per
category:

```text
| Rank | Species          | Specimens | Percent (%) |
| ---- | ---------------- | --------- | ----------- |
| 1    | Bunomys coelestis| 24        | 40.00       |
| 2    | Bunomys penitus  | 18        | 30.00       |
```

The second column header follows the grouping you selected, and the count
column header follows the measure. With a breakdown active, the series column
sits between them, and categories with no recorded value are exported as
`Not recorded`.

## Spatial statistics

The spatial panel counts `Specimens`, `Species`, or `Family` per site
coordinate. `Coordinates by species` asks you to pick a species first and then
counts that species at each coordinate.

The spatial table exports one row per coordinate with the place name, locality,
latitude, longitude, elevation, count, and percent. This is the fastest way to
get a coordinate list with effort attached to it for a permit report or for a
GIS program.

## Writing the file

The export dialog is the same for both panels. On a phone it opens as a bottom
sheet, and on a wider screen as a dialog.

{% steps %}

1. Check the file name. NAHPU proposes one built from the project name, the
   measure and grouping, the active filter, and today's date.

2. Turn on `Append date` if you want the current date added to the name. This
   helps when you export the same table repeatedly during a field season.

3. Choose the format. `CSV` and `TSV` are the safest for analysis, `Excel`
   writes an `.xlsx` workbook, and `JSON` keeps the header names as keys.

4. Select a destination folder. On desktop the file is written where you
   choose; on mobile NAHPU uses the app export folder.

5. Select `Export`. When the file is written, the button becomes `Share` so you
   can send the table straight to another app.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="note" %}

The export writes the rows currently in the table, so the measure, grouping,
breakdown, and filter all carry through to the file. Change the controls and
export again when you need a different cut of the data.

{% /aside %}

